From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: domicile CI <ci@domicile.invalid>
Date: Fri, 25 Sep 2026 16:57:42 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] domicile: the browser turns and scales a monitor's window

A monitor on its side came up right only because the page turned itself:
`<Screen>` drew each region through a CSS rotate-and-scale, and a shell
had to map every pointer position and warp through the same turn. What
lived outside a region -- a portal, the launcher's dialog -- came out
sideways, and the pointer's arrow and its travel were the panel's.

ChromeOS never asks a page to do this, and the parts it uses are all
here. So the compositor's layout says how each connector is turned and
scaled, `DrmScreen` puts both on the display, and a views window on that
display does what ash does with a rotated panel:

- its root transform turns DIPs onto the panel's pixels, so the root
  window is laid out upright and events arrive upright;
- the compositor is handed the turn as a display transform hint, so the
  frame is rendered upright and turned on its way to the plane;
- the display's scale is the window's device scale factor, so a page's
  CSS pixels are the desktop's logical ones and render sharp.

Only on a platform that says so -- `turns_windows_with_their_display`,
which DRM alone sets. A windowed platform's screen is turned by its
window system, and a window turning itself there would be turned twice.
Screen rectangles still convert by the scale alone: the turn is about a
window's own corner.

The pointer follows too. `CursorController` is compiled off ChromeOS so
`DrmCursor::MoveCursor` turns a hand's travel with the monitor it is on,
and `DrmWindowHost` tells it which way whenever a window moves or a
display turns under it. The arrow itself turns because `wm::CursorLoader`
reads the display's rotation, which it now has.
